  "823628:f63867ffaf7b89dd": {
    "starters_hash": "f63867ffaf7b89dd",
    "game_pk": 823628,
    "screening": {
      "analyst_assessments": [
        {
          "role": "pitching",
          "game_winner": "NYM",
          "reasoning": "Freddy Peralta brings a proven 5-6 inning profile with a 2.54 ERA over his last five starts and strikeout rates ranging from 15-30%. Elmer Rodr\u00edguez has zero trackable MLB data, forcing heavy regression toward a replacement-level projection (5.50+ ERA, 1.45+ WHIP). Time-through-order degradation hits unknown starters hardest\u2014rosters lack scouting on his secondary offerings, but hitters adjust quickly by the second and third pass. Peralta's fastball/slider mix generates weak contact and limits hard-hit barrels, providing a 0.75 to 1.0 run advantage through the first five innings."
        },
        {
          "role": "hitting",
          "game_winner": "NYY",
          "reasoning": "Both lineups are struggling (team averages .220-.235), but the Yankees possess superior power variance with Aaron Judge and Ben Rice combining for 11.2% HR rates. While Peralta is quality, he is homer-prone against left-handed power (Rice, Bellinger, Chisholm, McMahon). The Mets rely almost entirely on Juan Soto for elite production, and the bottom third of their order (Ewing 20 PA, Senger 0 PA, Baty .221 AVG) represents easy outs. Platoon advantage is neutral in a day game, but the Yankees' lineup depth provides more paths to a crooked number."
        },
        {
          "role": "bullpen",
          "game_winner": "NYM",
          "reasoning": "Both pens are fresh, but usage patterns favor the Mets. Peralta projects to complete six innings, allowing manager flexibility to deploy high-leverage arms in the 7th-8th against the Yankees' heart of the order. Conversely, Rodr\u00edguez will likely face the 18-21 batter threshold by the 4th inning, forcing early NYY bullpen exposure against Soto, Vientos, and Melendez with middle-relief arms. The Mets' bullpen enters with a structural advantage: shorter required usage and better rest distribution."
        },
        {
          "role": "environment",
          "game_winner": "NYM",
          "reasoning": "Citi Field's 0.96 park factor and 0mph wind create a pitcher's environment, suppressing home run distance by 8-12 feet compared to league average. The 72\u00b0F day-game temperature does not offset the park's expansive gaps or the heavy air. These conditions neutralize the Yankees' power advantage (Judge, Rice) more than the Mets' contact-oriented approach (Soto, Semien). The environment amplifies the value of the superior starting pitcher and points toward an under."
        },
        {
          "role": "market",
          "game_winner": "NYM",
          "reasoning": "The -110 line (52.5% implied) underweights the pitching disparity. Public money flows toward the Yankees brand, Aaron Judge, and the Subway Series rivalry narrative, keeping the Mets' price artificially low. Rodr\u00edguez's complete lack of data introduces asymmetric risk\u2014marketmakers price him as a 5.00+ ERA placeholder, but the true uncertainty band is wide (3.50-7.00 ERA). Given Peralta's 30-start sample versus Rodr\u00edguez's 0-start sample, the true probability should be 56-57%, creating value on the home side."
        },
        {
