      "analyst_assessments": [
        {
          "role": "pitching",
          "game_winner": "TOR",
          "reasoning": "Gausman provides a significantly higher floor with four quality starts (6+ IP, \u22642 ER) in his last five outings, featuring elite command (2 BB in 24 IP outside of the outlier blowup). Flaherty exhibits extreme volatility with three sub-4-inning starts in his last five, including a 2.0 IP disaster on 4/25. Flaherty's BB/9 is concerning (11 BB in 17.2 IP over last five), setting up early baserunners against a patient TOR lineup. COUNTER-CASE: Flaherty's 10-K upside game (5/6) shows frontline potential if his slider is sharp, and Gausman's 6-ER blowup on 5/11 proves he is not immune to early damage."
        },
        {
          "role": "hitting",
          "game_winner": "TOR",
          "reasoning": "While both lineups are offensively challenged (collective AVG <.240), TOR holds the platoon edge with multiple high-BB% hitters (Guerrero 10%, Schneider 12%) against Flaherty's walk-prone approach. Guerrero Jr. is due for positive regression on his .238 BABIP. DET's lineup lacks punch beyond Greene (.257 AVG) and Torkelson's power potential is neutralized by a 27.3% K-rate against Gausman's splitter. COUNTER-CASE: DET's superior team OBP (.310 vs .298) and home-field familiarity could lead to better at-bats against a TOR staff that has struggled with the long ball recently."
        },
        {
          "role": "bullpen",
          "game_winner": "TOR",
          "reasoning": "Bullpen advantage stems from starter length projection. Gausman is built for 6+ innings (96-100 pitch count tolerance), while Flaherty has averaged only 3.8 IP over his last five starts. This forces DET to cover 4-5 innings with their pen versus TOR's 3-4, increasing variance exposure for the home team in late-game situations. Both pens are fresh, but volume favors TOR. COUNTER-CASE: If Flaherty unexpectedly cruises through 5 IP efficiently, DET's bullpen availability becomes an asset rather than a liability, and TOR's middle relief has shown vulnerability in high-leverage spots."
        },
        {
          "role": "environment",
          "game_winner": "none",
          "reasoning": "Comerica Park's 0.96 run factor and spacious outfield suppresses extra-base hits, favoring pitchers. Calm 0mph wind eliminates carry, and 72\u00b0F is thermally neutral. Day games historically boost offense slightly, but the park's deadening effect and both lineups' high ground-ball tendencies should keep scoring down. Conditions are symmetric with no discernible advantage. COUNTER-CASE: Day-game visibility issues with the sky at Comerica could impact outfielder reads, potentially benefiting hitters on high fly balls that turn into doubles."
        },
        {
          "role": "market",
          "game_winner": "DET",
          "reasoning": "The market prices TOR at -115 (53.7%), slightly underestimating DET's home underdog value. Flaherty's volatility creates a wide distribution of outcomes where his 20th-percentile result is a disaster, but his 60th-percentile (the 10-K game) makes DET a live dog at +115. The public is overreacting to Gausman's name value versus Flaherty's ERA volatility. COUNTER-CASE: The line is efficient; TOR's superior roster construction and Gausman's consistency justify the slight favorite price, and DET's lineup has the 5th-worst wRC+ against RHP in the league."
        },
